1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Sigurd Haftorsson Giske til Sudrheim=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Hafthor Jonsson Jonsen, af Sudreim and Agnes Haakonsdatter
2 CONT
2 CONT =A line through Sigurd Havtorsson is one of very few documented lines from Norwegian viking Kings to present.=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sigurd Havtoresson was a Norwegian knight , magistrate , chieftain and governor . He was the son of Havtore Jonsson and Agnes Håkonsdotter , who was the daughter of King Håkon V.
2 CONT * Sigurd Havtoresson and his wife Ingebjørg left behind two children, their son Håkon, who died without heirs in 1407, and their daughter Agnes. She married the Swedish-born Jon Marteinsson, and it was their son, the state chancel
2 CONC lor Sigurd Jonsson , who was left with the large estate of his grandfather.
2 CONT * In letters from 1343 ( Diplomatarium Norvegicum , DN, vol. 2 no. 258) and 1355 ( DN, vol. 1 no. 345) Sigurd Havtoresson used two different seals . In addition, he used his own seal as a magistrate in Oslo in 1347 ( DN, vol. 1 no
2 CONC . 307) and 1348 ( DN, vol. 4 no. 334).
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family and Property=
2 CONT
2 CONT As the sons of Haakon V, Sigurd and his brother Jon Havtoresson (often called the Havtoressønene) had a family background unlike any other Norwegian nobleman. After their father, they inherited a large estate, which was divided so
2 CONC  that Sigurd took over his father's estate Sørum ( Sudreim ) and the bulk of the estate at Romerik . Jon had his main share assigned to Borgarsysla , concentrated on the large estates Huseby in Onsøy and Borregård in Tune .
2 CONT
2 CONT The estates increased through inheritance, accelerated by the development after the Black Death . This brought two large estates to the Havtoressønene: all or part of the estate of the knight Sigurd Erlendsson at Sandbu in Vågå an
2 CONC d his wife Gudrun Ivarsdotter – probably the daughter of Ivar Jonsson, uncle of the Havtoressønene – who died from (settlement 1353), and the estate of their cousin Herdis Torvaldsdotter , including property in Shetland (judgment 
2 CONC 1366). However, it does not appear that they received any of the inheritance of Ogmund Finnsson , even though Lord Ogmund, by his own statement, did not recognize any other heirs than Lord Jon and Lord Sigurd.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1342, Sigurd received a papal dispensation to marry maiden Ingebjørg, daughter of Erling Vidkunnsson . A dispensation that was necessary since they were relatives in the fourth degree. Erling's only son Bjarne died during or sh
2 CONC ortly after the Black Death, and when Erling passed away in 1355, Sigurd and his wife Ingebjørg became the main heirs. Erling had transferred the main estate Giske to Ingebjørg the previous year, his son Bjarne had asked him to do
2 CONC  so while he was on his last days. With the Giske estate came the northern Norwegian estates that Erling Vidkunnsson left behind, with the old large estates Bjarkøy , Tjøtta and Dønnes as centers. From now on, Sigurd Havtoresson w
2 CONC as Norway's richest man, and Giske became the main estate in the estate complex.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Political Activity=
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebellion against King Magnus Eriksson
2 CONT Sigurd was among the circle of nobles who rebelled against King Magnus Eriksson both in 1333, when he could hardly have been more than 18 years old, and in 1338–1339. Erling Vidkunnsson was the leader of the first uprising, in 133
2 CONC 8–1339 Sigurd was a leader together with Ivar Ogmundsson and Bjarne Erlingsson . A papal letter of 1334 states that Sigurd participated in taking Bishop Hallvard of Hamar prisoner and robbing him of his estates and a castle. He wa
2 CONC s excommunicated , but released from the excommunication. It is uncertain whether the controversy was politically motivated.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Knight and Lawyer=
2 CONT
2 CONT After the reconciliation in 1339, there is no direct evidence of conflict between the king and Sigurd, but there are indications that King Magnus initially kept Sigurd somewhat at a distance. At Varberg Castle in 1343, when King M
2 CONC agnus made the squires Håkon the Norwegian king ( Håkon VI ), Sigurd, Bjarne Erlingsson and Ogmund Finnsson were present with squires status . Four years later, when the will of King Magnus and Queen Blanca was drawn up, Ogmund ha
2 CONC d become a knight , and Sigurd is placed second in the squires, after Bjarne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd and Bjarne must have been knighted soon after the will was drawn up (between 15.7.1347 and 16.2.1348). For Sigurd, it may have happened at the same time as he became a magistrate in Oslo (first mentioned as a magistrate on 
2 CONC 18.8.1347). His brother Jon, who was about two years older, had then been a knight for 10 years. Sigurd probably had some use for the magistrate's office after 1350, and he was then governor of Southern Hedmark for a few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Chieftain and Sheriff=
2 CONT
2 CONT The strong property interests Sigurd acquired in Western Norway and Northern Norway may explain his taking over the prestigious position of chieftain at Bergenhus . He was in open conflict with the Hanseatics in 1365 and 1366, and
2 CONC  in 1370 Hanseatic attacks on Mr. Sigurd are mentioned. That year he was staying in Oslo, and during the plague that ravaged the city, Mrs. Ingebjørg apparently died. Sigurd was probably then the governor of Romerike ; he had a sh
2 CONC eriff here in 1376.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following period, it seems that Sigurd concentrated on the administration of the large collection of estates, which were severely affected in the crisis period after the Black Death . But he had a prominent place in the kin
2 CONC g's council. In the peace treaty with the Hanseatic League in 1369, he is the only nobleman besides the king mentioned as the king's advisor. In several contexts, including in 1381 when the child king Olav IV was praised at the Øy
2 CONC rating , he is mentioned as the king's kinsman (relative). However, he never achieved the throne . A letter from 1390, which concerned the renewal of an earlier land grant, must preferably be interpreted as meaning that Sigurd was
2 CONC  then dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
