2 CONT '''Page 111'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Diugelrich. Land ownership in Steneby parish and Wedbo district on Dal, Elfsborg county, consisted in 1858 of Södra Dingelvik Vg and Norra Dingelvik '^^.q mantal estate with '/g mantal Jusketorp, '/g mantal Östra Mon and Vg ment. 
2 CONC Planted, raw and reed, with a neat farm building, seed of 25 barrels of autumn and 120 barrels of spring grain, except potatoes, and belonged to the deceased mill patron Henry de Reverony St. Cyr's bankruptcy estate. Norra Dingelv
2 CONC ik is an old farm, whose name in 1449, when it was owned by the squire Sven Jonsson, was written Dyngowiik. In 1563, the farm belonged to a well-born wife Karin, who in addition to both Dingelviksgårds owned several other homestea
2 CONC ds in the parish. In 1577, Norra Dingelvik belonged to Nils Månsson pä Bro, 16 04 wife Margaretha Svenske, 17 83 the hotjunker Reuter, 1809 the horseman Sam. Kafle, 1825 Crown Sheriff Wennerlöf, 1827 Carl Pet. Åberg, 1850 Baron St
2 CONC . Cyr and Countess Ramel, née Lewenhaupt. Södra Dingelvik has mostly had the same owners as Norra, but was owned in 1681 by Major Ahlfeldt, in 17 68 by mill patron Sam. Chef.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://arkivkopia.se/bok/runeberg-ladal Arkivkopia Runeberg]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dingelvik norre 1 qvarn. D:o south 1 saw. Högen 1 d:o. Læstviken 1 qvarn. Katrineholm ironworks at S. Dingelvik.Rå och Rör. y$ Gesslanda. 's Jusketorp.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Skrefs Atlebyn and Atlebön in the 1550s and 1570s in the tithe lengths.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Called Prayer by the common people.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Named Nolbyn in 1605 and was then recorded as a church plot. Rlef not assigned number of homes before 1635, under the title church homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Commonly called Relen.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Called the old seat farm in 1681 and was then owned by the major Sv. Liedeman.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Now called Kristineberg. In 1681 it also bore the name of an old manor house. Owned then by wife Karin Svenske: 1683 by Ingehorg Silfversvärd. Yar Linnart Svinhufvud's estate in 1664. Bought from the provost Nils Hessel-gren in
2 CONC  1697 by the county chief J. Leijonbjelke for 510 r:dr curant, d. eh. 765 dalers silver coins. Built in 1805 by the provost L. Wallstedt; 1850 by commission surveyor H. Marin. m. fl.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) Skrefs Dyngowiik 1449. Owned then by the squire Sven Jonsson, and thus an ancient manor. See a morning gift letter issued by him at Grinstad parish, No. 8. The same Sven Jonsson is mentioned in a letter on 10 February. 1494 (pr
2 CONC inted by Lilljegren after the original in v. Engeströmska library, 1819. page 41). published by Magnus Bonde's widow Kadrin Ingwarsdotter. Owned in 1563 by the well-to-do wife Karin, who owned both Dingelviks farms. Lästviken, Åse
2 CONC n, Högen, Hölgerud, Hafden, Bågen, etc. fl. In 1577, Nils Månsson at Bro was the owner of this: 1664 wife Margareta Svenske: 1783 court junior Reuter: 1809 horse master Sam. Tile: 1827 Carl Pet. Åberg: 1845 crown county in Näs dis
2 CONC trict of Wermland JF Wennerlöf: 18-17 baron St. Gyr and countess Ramel, born Lewenhaupt.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8) Has mostly had the same owner, as Norra Dingelvik. But owned in 1681 by Major Gregor. Ahlfclt, 1768 by the utility patron Sam. Chef.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9) Has also had the name Ekarebol. Called new manor in 1681. Owned then by Linnart Svinhufvud: 1783 by Sergeant Svinlmfvud: 1850 by C. Fr. Wow.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10) Old manor house. See X:o 7. Egdes 1664 by wife Margareta Svenske: 1686 by ensign Lars Svinhufvud: 1600 by Luiradshöfdingen Joh. Rho-din. who bought this manor from the latter for 500 riksdaler curant. 1 in later times belonged
2 CONC  to the owners of Billingsholm ironworks.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11) See i\:o 7. Was in 1664 well-born Patrik Tbomson's seat farm; 1681 Mrs. Agneta S\renskes: *Häradshöfdingcn. the assessor de Freses; In 1809 the commissioner was
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.geni.com/photo/view/5326616626820068026?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000191744977823 Dingelvik 2:118 Dingelvik Estate]
2 CONT
2 CONT Larger farm complex located on a hill in an open agricultural landscape (see map 5). On the property there is a residential building, wing building, warehouse, barn, stables and wagon slides.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dingelvik is a large manor house with a long history. In the past, there were 46 crofts under the property, and farms such as Baldersnäs, Billingsfors, Värnshögen, Långed and Lästevik. The farm is mentioned as early as '''1449 in 
2 CONC written sources, then with the squire Sven Jonsson as owner. During the latter part of the 16th century, Dingelvik's two farms (North and South) were owned by the well-to-do Mrs. Karin Svensdotter. In the 17th century, the farm ca
2 CONC me to belong to Margareta Svenske, and towards the end of the 18th century, courtier Reuter became the owner. During the 19th and 20th centuries, the farm has had about four dozen different owners.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The residential building probably has an old core, but has been rebuilt in stages, and underwent a major rebuild in 1848. It was probably then that it got its second floor. The building was renovated during the first half of the 2
2 CONC 0th century. Recently, the building received a pillared porch on the south side. The barn and the stables are large, well-preserved and estate-like buildings with brick and natural stone walls. The magazine is also a larger buildi
2 CONC ng with well-maintained older features. In addition to the main building, these three outbuildings have great significance for the farm's cultural-historical value, as well as the fact that the farm as a whole has been of great im
2 CONC portance in the area since long ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order for the cultural-historical value to be maintained, it is essential that, among other things, the following are preserved: The structure of the farm with the shape and proportions of the various buildings. The facades of 
2 CONC the main building in light discolored plaster, older doors and windows, the profile of the eaves cornice and the single-cupped clay tiles of the roof.
2 CONT
2 CONT The facade of the magazine in vertical cover strip panel painted in red mud color, older doors and windows. The ashlar foundation of the barn, older doors and windows including frames. The stable's brick walls, smooth panels paint
2 CONC ed in red mud color, as well as older doors and windows, brick window surrounds
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://start.varmlandsrotter.se/varmlandsanor/artiklar/1999_1/ A Major Property Owner in Värmland and Dalsland, By Lennart Paulsson]
2 CONT
2 CONT Margareta Persdotter was a Large landowner in Värmland and Dalsland. She inherited salvation property in Steneby parish on Dal after her mother Karin Andersdotter. After the death of her father Per Larsson's "Little Per", she also
2 CONC  inherited the farm estate Bro in Värmland, where she and her husband Nils Månsson Roos are believed to have lived from 1579. After the death of her husband in 1603, Margareta ruled and placed her own domains alone. Only in Steneb
2 CONC y under the Dingelvik farm she had 12 saviors. It was not only in Steneby that she owned properties. According to a protocol from 1619, she owned the entire Sund's farm in the parish of Nössemarks on Dal as well as several salvati
2 CONC on homes in Värmland. Margareta Persdotter died childless in Bro around 1629 and as the closest heir, Elin Jacobsdotter of rider champion Lennart Persson Svenske.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a protocol from 1619, the entire Sunds farm was owned by Margareta Pedersdotter on Bro. She was probably married to a Pär Nilsson in Bro. He then owned Bro's farm and several other homes in Värmland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Already in this quote I see an error. Margareta was not married to Pär Nilsson but to Nils Månsson Roos. In addition, Margareta died childless and had no breast wings.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next quote reads: Pär Nilsson and Maret Nilsdotter were probably full siblings. Maret inherited his mother's ownership in Sund and had it until 1623.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1623, Maret Nilsdotter sold his ownership to Björn Nilsson in Sund. I believe that this heritage castle was sold within the family and can reasonably assume that Björn was the brother of Maret and Pär.
2 CONT
2 CONT If I can believe without having any evidence, then Maret, Pär and Björn were children of Nils Månsson Roos in a previous marriage and in this way Maret and Björn may have inherited the property in Sund in Nössemark while Nils inhe
2 CONC rited in Bro. For several years I have tried to clarify this for several years, but each time I have to give up. Maybe someone can help me figure this out, I'm grateful to have a relationship with both Margareta Persdotter and Mar
2 CONC et Nilsdotter and Björn Nilsson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The following statement shows what I know about Margareta Persdotter:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Margareta Persdotter'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Large landowner in Värmland and Dalsland. She inherited freehold property in Steneby parish on Dal from her mother Karin Andersdotter. After the death of her father Per Larsson's "Little Per", she also inherited the Bro manor in V
2 CONC ärmland, where she and her husband Nils Månsson Roos are said to have stayed from 1579. After her husband's death in 1603, Margareta ruled and ruled alone over her domains. In Steneby alone under the Dingelvik estate, she had 12 r
2 CONC escue homes. It was not only in Steneby that she owned properties. According to a protocol from 1619, she owned the entire Sund farm in the parish of Nössemark in Dal, as well as a number of salvation homes in Värmland. Margareta 
2 CONC Persdotter died childless in Bro around 1629 and Elin Jacobsdotter Snakeborg Bååt, wife of master rider Lennart Persson Svenske, registered as the nearest heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Nils Månsson Roos'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Margareta Persdotter's husband Nils Månsson Roos, like his son, belonged to the cavalry of his father Karl IX. Died in 1603 in Charles IX's campaign in Livonia. He was the son of Måns Knutsson Roos of Hjelmsäter. Belonged to Charl
2 CONC es IX's horsemen and sat on the lordly seat of Blolmberg in Västergötland. Married to Christina Gregorsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Per Larsson (Little)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Margareta Persdotter's father Per Larsson  was a bailiff based at the Bro manor in Värmland. Born in 1522 in Östbro, Bro and died there in 1577. Married to Karin Andersdotter, died in 1576.
2 CONT
